  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Oregon received $1.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $913M, making the Department of Defense the largest buyer in the state. Obligations are down 23.4% year over year, with 7,747 awards and an average award size of $206K. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $581M. VIGOR MARINE LLC is the largest vendor, with $567M in obligations. Fiscal-year comparisons show a peak at $2.5B in FY23, followed by $2.4B in FY24, $2.1B in FY25, and $1.6B in FY26. The Department of Agriculture obligated $190M and the Department of Transportation obligated $139M in FY2026 to date. Other sizable industries include Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $148M and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $93.3M. Construction and maritime activity account for multiple high-dollar industries and vendors. All figures are contract obligations, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the FY2026 spending total in Oregon?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense drove the FY2026 total by obligating $913M. Ship Building and Repairing and VIGOR MARINE LLC also concentrated dollars, with $581M and $567M respectively. Other sectors and buyers contributed smaller shares, including the Department of Agriculture with $190M and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction with $148M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 compare with prior years in Oregon?",
        "body": "Obligations in FY2026 are $1.6B, lower than FY25 at $2.1B and the FY23 peak at $2.5B. Obligations are down 23.4% year over year. Award volume and the average award size reflect the partial fiscal year totals, with 7,747 awards and an average award size of $206K."
      }
    ]
  },
